# howto.domain-discovery

surface the domain's true objects (dobjs — `Surfer`, `SurfLesson`; shape `[...noun][status]?`) and operations (dops — `genSurfLesson`, `setSurferSkillLevel`; shape `[verb][...noun]`) before you declare them in code. loop the moves, expand then collect, until the terms converge:
1. listen — adopt the exact words of the expert (the canonical term) and the layfolk (the intuitive one, which shows where jargon obscures). they agree → a strong candidate; they diverge → a synonym or overload to settle. adopt, never translate
2. name from the motive — why it exists, not how it runs
3. ask why five times — each why strips a layer of mechanism; stop at the answer that no longer shifts, the buried motive. "reservation record" → "reserve a slot" → "a surfer keeps a spot in a lesson" = `SurfLesson`, dop `genSurferSpot`
4. decompose dimensionally — walk the product of orthogonal axes; axes = attributes, cells = objects, forbidden cells = invariants (howto.dimensional-decomposition)
5. tell one concrete story end to end, then distill it to given/when/then — nouns = dobjs, events = dops, status changes = attributes. the narrative discovers; the bdd refines. it catches the dops a static object list misses
6. probe the lifecycle — each status and the event that drives each move
7. hunt the relationships — refs, back-refs, the natural key
8. vet the term — no synonym, no overload; one word per concept
premier tactics: 4 and 5. the test and the caveat: def.domain-discovery
see also: rule.require.domain-discovery-for-term-proposals · rule.require.persist-domain-term-evidence · ref.domain-discovery.fundamentals (evans, korzybski, toyota, brandolini, north, zwicky)
full brief: `howto.domain-discovery.md`
